// how a Trainer explains a competitive team (explainTeam in a6f_talk.js): each archetype's plan (ARCHETYPES in a6g_teams.js), its
// sub-archetypes, and the job of each role. A role's job is only claimed when the Pokémon really has the move or Ability for it
// (need: "mv" its role move, "ab" its role Ability); otherwise it's described by its best attack. Keep these in step with the battle rules.
const ARCH_PLAN = {
  rain: "Rain Dance starts rain for 5 rounds (8 with a Damp Rock): Water moves hit ×1.5 and Fire moves ×0.5, Swift Swim gives Speed +2, and Thunder and Hurricane never miss",
  sun: "a Drought or Orichalcum Pulse Pokémon brings harsh sunlight as it comes in, for 5 rounds (8 with a Heat Rock): Fire moves hit ×1.5 and Water moves ×0.5, Chlorophyll gives Speed +2, and Solar Beam and Solar Blade fire the same turn at ×1.5",
  sand: "a Sand Stream Pokémon whips up a sandstorm as it comes in, for 5 rounds (8 with a Smooth Rock): everything but Rock, Ground and Steel types loses 1/16 of its HP a turn, Rock, Ground and Steel types get Sp. Def +1, Sand Rush gives Speed +2 and Sand Force powers up Rock, Ground and Steel moves",
  snow: "a Snow Warning Pokémon brings the snow as it comes in (hail and snow are one weather here), for 5 rounds (8 with an Icy Rock): everything but Ice types loses 1/16 of its HP a turn, Ice types get Defense +1, Blizzard never misses, and Aurora Veil can go up",
  trickroom: "Trick Room flips the turn order for 5 rounds, so the slowest Pokémon move first and slow heavy hitters do the damage; the setter puts it up the moment it comes in, without using its turn, and its Room Service makes it last 8 rounds. The second setter waits on the bench until the room is down: coming in while it's up would end it",
  tailwind: "Tailwind gives the whole team Speed +2 for 4 rounds and the Pokémon out there +10% turn meter at once, so the attackers get their turns first; the setter has priority (Prankster or a priority move, and the Quick Claw) so it gets the wind going before the other side moves",
  ho: "hit hard and fast: screens or hazards from leads that move first, then setup sweepers boost and clean up before the other side gets going",
  bo: "strong attackers that can take a hit too, so the team wins the trades",
  balance: "a bit of everything: an Intimidate, a bulky Pokémon with recovery, entry hazards, and strong attackers to win with",
  stall: "screens, status, team healing and walls with recovery wear the other side down slowly with burns, poison and entry hazards",
  phazing: "entry hazards go up, then Dragon Tail, Circle Throw, Roar and Whirlwind keep dragging the foes out: every one dragged in takes the hazards again, loses its stat boosts and starts with an empty turn meter, while bulky Pokémon and healing hold on",
  redirect: "the redirector comes in first and its Follow Me, Rage Powder or Spotlight goes off by itself the moment it's out, without using its turn, pulling the attacks away while its partners set up for free. It's bulky on purpose: the redirect lasts for as long as it stays out",
  electric: "an Electric Surge or Hadron Engine Pokémon sets Electric Terrain as it comes in, for 5 rounds (8 with a Terrain Extender): Electric moves from Pokémon on the ground hit ×1.5, nobody on the ground can fall asleep, and Quark Drive, Surge Surfer and an Electric Seed's holder get going",
  psychic: "a Psychic Surge Pokémon sets Psychic Terrain as it comes in, for 5 rounds (8 with a Terrain Extender): Psychic moves from Pokémon on the ground hit ×1.5, Expanding Force hits every foe at ×1.5, and priority moves can't touch the team's grounded Pokémon",
  priority: "every priority move a Pokémon knows adds 10% turn meter for each point of priority the moment it comes in (Quick Attack +10%, Fake Out +30%, Protect +40%, Helping Hand +50%; a priority Ability and a Quick Claw too), and they stack, so the team moves first and its hardest hitters strike before the other side can act",
};
